## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, $p_n(x_n) \to a(x)$.

**Key fact (uniform convergence on compact subsets).** Since $p_m$ is the $m$-th Taylor polynomial of $a$ and $p_m \to a$, the function $a$ is real-analytic. A standard property of Taylor series of analytic functions is that the Taylor polynomials converge to the function **uniformly on every compact subset** of the (open) interval/disk of convergence. In particular, for any point $x$ in the interior of the domain of convergence and any $\delta > 0$ small enough that $[x-\delta, x+\delta]$ lies inside the domain, we have
$$\sup_{z \in [x-\delta, x+\delta]} |p_m(z) - a(z)| \longrightarrow 0 \quad \text{as } m \to \infty.$$

**Continuity of $a$.** Each $p_m$ is a polynomial and hence continuous. Since $p_m \to a$ uniformly on compact sets, the limit $a$ is continuous (uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous).

**Setting up the diagonal estimate.** Since $x_n \to x$, choose $\delta > 0$ such that $K := [x - \delta,\, x + \delta]$ is a compact subset of the domain of convergence. There exists $N_0$ such that for all $n \geq N_0$, $x_n \in K$.

We decompose the error into two parts:
$$p_n(x_n) - a(x) = \underbrace{\bigl(p_n(x_n) - a(x_n)\bigr)}_{\text{(I)}} + \underbrace{\bigl(a(x_n) - a(x)\bigr)}_{\text{(II)}}.$$

**Term (II) vanishes:** Since $a$ is continuous (established above) and $x_n \to x$,
$$|a(x_n) - a(x)| \longrightarrow 0.$$

**Term (I) vanishes:** For every $n \geq N_0$, the point $x_n$ lies in $K$, so
$$|p_n(x_n) - a(x_n)| \leq \sup_{z \in K} |p_n(z) - a(z)| \longrightarrow 0$$
by the uniform convergence of $p_n$ to $a$ on the compact set $K$.

**Combining:** By the triangle inequality,
$$|p_n(x_n) - a(x)| \leq \sup_{z \in K}|p_n(z) - a(z)| + |a(x_n) - a(x)| \longrightarrow 0 + 0 = 0.$$

Therefore $p_n(x_n) \to a(x)$. $\blacksquare$

**Remark on the necessity of uniform convergence.** The argument relies crucially on $p_m \to a$ being **uniform** on a neighborhood of $x$ (not merely pointwise). For Taylor polynomials of an analytic function, this uniform-on-compact-subsets convergence is automatic within the radius of convergence, so the conclusion holds. Under mere pointwise convergence (which does not arise for Taylor series of analytic functions inside the radius of convergence), the conclusion can fail in general—uniform convergence on a neighborhood of $x$ is the essential hypothesis.

